// **Food a player put in by hand** -- what the lab's FOOD brush paints.
// Owner, 2026-10-03: *"there is a food tool ... it creates windfall and that
// seems to degrade very quickly. It shouldn't degrade or disappear at all."*
//
// **Why a material of its own rather than a flag on the brush.** Decay is
// material-driven (`decay.rs` reads `decays_into` off the cell's material and
// nothing else), so the only way to paint food that never rots, without
// touching the rot schedule every natural `windfall` cell runs on, is a
// material with no `decays_into` at all. Natural windfall keeps rotting
// exactly as before; a run that never touches the brush never meets this
// file.
//
// **Everything else is `windfall.ron`, copied, not re-argued** -- same
// density, slope, flammability, softness and, above all, the same
// `food_energy` and `food_class`, so an ant's gut reads a painted cell
// exactly as it read the windfall the brush used to lay. Read that file for
// why each value is what it is. The two differences:
//
// - **No `decays_into`.** It stays until something eats it, burns it or it
//   is erased.
// - **No seed inside.** The brush always painted ownerless windfall, so no
//   painted cell ever carried a seed; a material of its own also stops those
//   cells reaching `World::windfall_bitten_ownerless`, a counter that exists
//   to find an upstream bug in *natural* windfall and was being fed by the
//   player's brush.
(
    name: "provisions",
    display: "Food",
    kind: Powder,
    density: 1.05,
    friction_angle: 26.0,
    // `windfall.ron`'s palette: the player asked for the food they already
    // knew, not a new-looking thing.
    colors: [
        (160, 40, 34),
        (120, 26, 36),
        (84, 42, 94),
        (40, 38, 64),
        (182, 92, 30),
        (176, 146, 48),
    ],
    flammability: 0.2,
    burn_temperature: 470.0,
    burn_duration: 40,
    burns_into: "ash",
    heat_conductivity: 0.2,
    insubstantial: true,
    falls_through_organisms: true,
    food_energy: 960.0,
    penetration_resistance: 0.05,
    food_class: -1.0,
)
